title: "[GT.with_locale()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label .doc-label-method}"
body-classes: doc-api-page
page-navigation: false
html-table-processing: none
---

::: {.doc-subject}

Set a column to be the default locale.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/posit-dev/great-tables/blob/main/great_tables/_modify_rows.py#L114-L164){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-Kind.FUNCTION}
```python
GT.with_locale(locale=None)
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

Setting a default locale affects formatters like `fmt_number()`, and `fmt_date()`,
by having them default to locale-specific features (e.g. representing one thousand
as 1.000,00)
:::

## Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[locale]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   An optional locale identifier that can be used for formatting values according the locale's
    rules. Examples include `"en"` for English (United States) and `"fr"` for French (France).
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [GT]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   The GT object is returned. This is the same object that the method is called on so that we
    can facilitate method chaining.
:::

## Examples {.doc-examples}

Let's create a table and set its `locale=` to `"ja"` for Japan. Then, we call `fmt_currency()`
to format the `"currency"` column. Since we didn't specify a `locale=` for `fmt_currency()`,
it will adopt the globally set `"ja"` locale.

::: {#986656a0 .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
from great_tables import GT, exibble


(
    GT(exibble)
    .with_locale("ja")
    .fmt_currency(
        columns="currency",
        decimals=3,
        use_seps=False
    )
)
```

